What this covers
----------------

CC0 applies to the urn:solid term registry contents:

  - <Name>/index.json   — each term's source-of-truth JSON-LD record
  - context.jsonld      — the canonical urn-solid JSON-LD context
  - corpus.jsonl        — generated: every term as one JSON-LD record per line
  - index.json          — generated: name → term lookup
  - reverse-index.json  — generated: upstream IRI → urn:solid term
  - schema/term.schema.json — meta-schema describing a term file

What this does NOT cover
------------------------

Build scripts and other code remain licensed under AGPL-3.0 (see LICENSE):

  - scripts/*.js
  - render.js
  - style.css
  - generated <Name>/index.html, top-level index.html, etc.

The split keeps the *vocabulary* a true commons (citable and reusable
in any product, proprietary or open) while the *implementation* of
the registry stays copyleft-protected.

Why
---

Vocabularies are infrastructure. The W3C/IANA convention is to license
vocabulary content permissively so the world can adopt the terms
without inheriting the registry's code license. CC0 matches what
Wikidata and many W3C vocabularies do.
